Recently there was discussion about nethacks's literary and
mythological influences. Moorcock and Tolkien came out as the main
contributors, obviously, and D&D (which itself borrows heavily from
Tolkien). There are dozens of other references, of course: Towels from
Hitch-Hiker's Guide, the Tourist-class references Pratchett, and who
knows how many others. Slashem takes it even further with references to
Star Wars, Cthulu mythos, Buffy/Blade and others. (Please feel free to
help me complete the above lists, by the way.)

My question though is this: What other sources do you think Nethack
should reference?  What books, films, games, comics, mytholgical and/or
religious/cultural sources would fit particularly well into nethack?
Why?

Here are a few things that spring to my mind:

- 8BIT Theatre: ( http://www.nuklearpower.com/latest.php ) Being
(mostly) set in the fantasy genre helps, of course, but 8Bit's humour
is just very suitable. I've been campaigning here for a sword-chucks
patch for a long time, simply because the idea of a weapon that
immolates the wielder just as effectively as the target is beautifully
nethackish. It's also a veritable fountain of YAFM-material, and
nethack could definitely use an armoire of invulnerability too.
(Artifact chest, proofed against g-cubes and the like, grants
protection points when carried, but is so outrageously heavy that only
an idiot would try to fight when holding it.)

- Dudley's dungeon: ( http://www.nicolaas.net/dudley ) Why shouldn't
nethack pay tribute to the nethack-tribute webcomic? All we need is
YAFM when someone starts a character named "Dudley".

- More HitchHiker's Guide: Specifically, more towels. I've already
posted on this subject (
http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_frm/thread/8f3ec618f39f231d/812752dfeffd6d60?q=towels+useful&rnum=1#812752dfeffd6d60
), so I won't say any more here.

- His Dark Materials: The wonderful trilogy of novels by Phillip
Pullman. It's been suggested before, so I won't go into too much
detail, but there are some fantastic ideas in there that would fit
nethack very well. The subtle knife would probably be too powerful as
an artifact, but smaller references- adding bears in plate armour, for
instance- would be great.

- Wesnoth. ( http://www.wesnoth.org ) Another great fantastic
fantasy-genre game released as open-source. I like to think of it as a
kind of younger sibling (or at least cousin) to nethack. Perhaps
nethack could make some small reference to it as a gesture of support
and solidarity- maybe an NPC named Delfador somewhere on the wizard's
quest?

- Scud: Disposible Assassin: ( http://www.scud.com/ ) If I could write
code, I'd write a vending machine patch. If I wrote a vending machine
patch, I'd follow it up with a Scud patch. For those who don't know
(most of you, probably) the basic premise is that you insert some coins
into a vending machine, and out steps a Tarantino-esque gun-toting
robotic assassin who promptly explodes upon successful termination of
the target. Great stuff. The author, Rob Schrab, has since gone on to
even better things ( http://www.channel101.com )

- Gary Larson: While there's nothing particularly nethackish about Gary
Larson (I don't think he ever once touched upon the fantasy genre in
his comics) someone did suggest a while ago that repeatedly trying to
open a locked door should produce the message "Welcome to Midvale
School fo the Gifted." Beautiful. Lots of potential for dog/cat YAFMs
too:
Fido appears to be writing something in the dust --more--
cAt fuD this waY -~>

- Schlock Mercenary. ( http://www.schlockmercenary.com ) Hard to
justify this one, but I am a complete schlock-evangelist. Surely
there's room in nethack for an "ominous hum" and some quotes from the
'seven habits'. And slashem already has koalas, so it's a short step to
O'benn warriors.

- Iain M. Banks: Again, I'm just crowbarring some of my favourite
fiction in here, but wouldn't a lazy gun be a wonderful thing to have
in slashem?

So there are mine- for now anyway. A surprising amount of comic
references actually. I do read real books, honest.
I'm sure I'll come back with more as an when. Who would like to add to
the list?



